WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.740
On what planets are we most likely to find
life?

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.770
Our instinct might be to search for Earth-like
worlds when sifting through the vastness of

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:18.770
the cosmos.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.290
But what if that instinct is mistaken?

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:28.199
Perhaps life, in its unrelenting stubbornness,
can also be found in the churning atmosphere

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:33.830
above gas giants, or the oven-like temperatures
of a sun-scorched world.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:39.830
Such incredible forms of life are explored
in Project C, a speculative biology epic by

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:44.620
the incredible artist Charlotte Veldhuizen,
whom you can follow and support in the links

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:45.620
below.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.649
The project is set within a vast congregation
of stars known as The Cluster, where life

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:54.360
has emerged upon numerous extreme worlds.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.030
So, for this entry into the archive, we can
embark together on a journey across the cosmos

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:06.850
— and explore just some of the worlds that
wait within The Cluster…

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.920
Travelling into the cluster, the first world
on our voyage is a Gas Giant.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.250
From space, the swirling currents of the planet’s
outer atmosphere make it impossible to predict

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.210
what lies below the clouds.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.280
Time for a closer look…

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.960
Descending into the atmosphere, one of the
strangest ecosystems in The Cluster comes

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.229
into view.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:31.799
Life on Ondriel has evolved to steer through
the cloudscape using gas-filled organs and

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:33.970
chemical jet propulsion.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:39.450
Like fish within an aerial ocean, life on
this planet has diversified into a vast array

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.970
of unique niches.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:47.960
Furthest from our position, Giant Gargantulillies
float above the layers of clouds, collecting

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.270
sunlight with their castle-sized discs.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:57.340
These floating florae are so big, they could
be considered entire ecosystems on their own.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.560
And in the foreground, a hunter ambushes its
prey.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:07.390
This curious organism possesses an exceptionally
wide field of vision thanks to eyes that sit

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.620
at the tips of long, maneuverable stalks.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:16.070
Like the stalks of snails, these eye-stalks
are retractable, and can be pulled in towards

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.750
the organism’s body during hazardous situations.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.860
The aerial hunter’s prey is surprised by
the sudden attack, but this equally strange

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:30.320
lifeform has a trick of their own — a spiked
barb that poses a serious threat to the thin

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.090
membrane of the predator’s floating bell.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:39.820
Like any ecosystem, this sky is a stage for
moves and countermoves in the great game of

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.480
life.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:47.250
The higher layers of Ondriel’s atmosphere
are rich in aero-algae — an abundant food

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:51.420
source that sustains a population of true
giants.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.340
Various whale-sized cylindrical organisms
spend their lives enjoying this bountiful

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.450
feast, feeding by maneuvering their gaping
mouth-analogs through clouds of floating algae.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:08.010
From a biological perspective, these lifeforms
are most comparable to the tube-shaped tunicates

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:13.329
of Earth — sessile lifeforms that feed by
filtering planktonic particles through their

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:14.329
bodies.

00:03:14.329 --> 00:03:20.480
The filter-feeders of Ondriel go a step further,
however, by being both heterotrophs and autotrophs

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.940
— meaning they are capable of both digestion
and photosynthesis to reach their colossal

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.450
sizes.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.250
Yet all environments have some form of apex
predator.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:37.420
In the deeper parts of Ondriel’s sky, the
menacing Abyssocaelid reigns supreme.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:42.190
One of the largest hunters anywhere in The
Cluster, this living dreadnaught feeds on

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.120
all manner of floating organisms in the stormy
depths.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:52.100
At risk of lightning strikes, the Abyssocaelid
possess organs that redirect electrical charge

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:57.810
away from the vitals… but these organic
lightning rods are not always effective.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:03.180
Sometimes, when lightning strikes in just
the right spot, the volatile gases in the

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.560
floating bell ignite.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.720
The resulting explosion spells the end for
the individual…

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.440
yet ingeniously, such eruptions also send
the creature’s spores into the air currents,

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.609
ensuring their lineage will live on…

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:23.150
And like the deep sea, the most extreme life
of all dwells in the dark sky many layers

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.450
below Ondriel’s surface.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:31.350
Here, the bristly, multi-pronged Windspears
contend with more lighting than the Abyssocaelids.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:37.650
But the electrosynthetic Windspears have become
one with the storm, having evolved to resist

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:42.620
the immense burst of energy in a lightning
strike, and can even use some of the deferred

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.730
energy to grow and reproduce.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:49.740
Exactly when such a lifeform first emerged
remains a mystery.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:55.470
Due to the gas giant's lack of fossil record,
it's tough to tell how the planet's evolutionary

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.250
tree fits together.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:02.790
Although its biodiversity suggests its roots
reach deep in time.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:08.930
Ondriel is a fascinating and extreme world,
but we must return to the stars once again,

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:14.630
and seek out other incredible biospheres…

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:20.510
The next planet on our voyage lies within
a Trinary star system, meaning it has three

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:21.510
suns.

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:27.370
As a result, the planet, called Terminus,
receives an extreme amount of UV-radiation.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:34.131
A tidally-locked world, one side of Terminus
exists in perpetual day, and the other in

00:05:34.131 --> 00:05:35.730
perpetual night.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.210
Yet life endures nonetheless...

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:44.120
Touching down near the sun-pole of Terminus,
we find a colony of tree-like organisms stretching

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.960
up towards the sky.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:52.300
In this blistering region, the direct solar
radiation eliminates anything but the hardiest

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:53.300
lifeforms.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:57.900
Analysis suggests these organisms are similar
to earth sponges.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:03.060
This makes sense, as experiments have shown
that certain sea sponges can survive massive

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:08.530
amounts of radiation — among the highest
of any multicellular animal.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:12.020
Under the eternal summer, not much else can
survive.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:17.310
To find more complex life, we’ll have to
journey further from the pole…

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:22.780
Due to an abundance of suspended water in
the atmosphere, permanent storms ravage much

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.000
of Terminus’s sunside.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.760
Yet on the rocky surface below the clouds,
an unusual assembly clings to life.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:35.860
These are Wind Gaspers — creatures whose
tough, clam-like shell helps keep them safe

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.090
from the unforgiving weather.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:43.430
While they might appear simple, Wind Gaspers
can survive many human lifetimes under the

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.630
eternal overhead gaze of the red sun.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:52.280
On Earth, certain species of giant clams can
live for a similar length of time — with

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.320
many exceeding 100 years.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:00.460
Wind Gaspers--forged by the planet’s many
extinction cycles-- are resilient enough to

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.300
withstand Terminus’s harsh winds.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:09.120
Indeed, these clever lifeforms have even learned
to use these winds to their advantage — feeding

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.130
off the nutrient-rich material that the permastorm
whips up.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:17.930
To survive on Terminus, one has to be resourceful…

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:23.950
Within the equatorial zone of Terminus, one
lifeform uses the winds for a different purpose.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:30.139
Thundershadows are titanic hunters who soar
above the equator in search of prey.

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:35.310
Surprisingly lightweight, their sail-like
wings catch the thermals of the storms, allowing

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:38.470
them to cover vast distances each day.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:44.770
Small, shelled animals graze on the ground
below, feeding on thick mats of bacteria clustered

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.380
around volcanic hotspots.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.330
A flash of overhead lightning illuminates
the circling Thundershadow.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:57.949
While passing through the equatorial zone,
we’ll be keeping one eye turned upwards…

00:07:57.949 --> 00:08:00.699
Yet not all of Terminus is so brutal.

00:08:00.699 --> 00:08:05.730
At some points of the season, certain regions
of the tidally-locked planet are downright

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:10.120
picturesque, and a welcome reprieve from the
wind and dust.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:15.810
Within Terminus’s liquid oceans, a stunning
array of biodiversity has emerged in response

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:18.430
to the balmy weather and gentle currents.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:24.730
Here, segmented lifeforms splash near the
surface of the shallows, pursuing tiny, eel-like

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:25.830
prey animals.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:30.650
While much of Terminus could be considered
a dreary planet, scenes like this one reveal

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:36.039
that even on an unpredictable world, incredible
sights can emerge.

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:42.690
It is tempting to stay longer, but it’s
time to continue our voyage...

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:48.080
The next planet on our voyage is more temperate,
and possesses a weaker gravitational pull

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:49.700
compared to earth.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.779
As we might imagine, the lesser gravity has
had a fascinating impact on the planet’s

00:08:54.779 --> 00:08:56.480
native life.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.600
Stepping out onto the surface, we find a startling
scene.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:06.360
With a great splash, a long-necked hunter
pulls a squid-like organism from the water.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:11.850
This scaly specimen is a Tanycroc, and they
are a unique-looking creature indeed.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:18.620
A shoreline ambush-predator, the Tanycroc
has converged on a niche similar to a crocodile’s

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.900
— just with an absurdly long neck.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:26.240
Planet Falora’s lower gravity relative to
earth means that such a lanky body plan is

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:27.630
quite effective.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:32.760
A precise plunge into the water can make short
work of any unlucky prey.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:38.490
In crystal-clear waters like these, nothing
evades the Tanycroc’s reach…

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:42.830
In the Faloran mangroves, the clear water
becomes murkier.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:49.100
Yet this dimness offers little anonymity for
the species who swim amongst the roots.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.500
Above the water’s surface, two keen-eyed
predators are on patrol.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:58.020
In the foreground, a jeweled thornykite carefully
chooses a target.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:03.450
These rare specialists catch their prey by
ambushing it from above — a strategy convergent

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:06.110
with Earth seabirds like gannets.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:12.120
In the background, the large-billed Basketmouth
scoops its beak full of plankton-filled water.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:17.710
Flamingos feed in a similar way, scooping
up small organisms from the water and straining

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.170
them through comb-like filaments.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:25.899
With these two specialists watching the murky
depths, there’s no place to hide…

00:10:25.899 --> 00:10:31.100
Out in deeper waters, prey animals have more
room to maneuver… but have to compete with

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.089
larger low-gravity predators.

00:10:33.089 --> 00:10:37.310
And a terror rules over the depths of the
outer swamp.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:43.700
Like many lifeforms on Faloran, at a glance,
the titanic Ichtyoserpens resembles an aquatic

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:44.700
reptile.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.950
Yet the Ichtyoserpens strangeness is betrayed
by their insides.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:54.010
Like the cold-water hagfish of Earth, the
Ichtyoserpens possess multiple hearts to pump

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:55.190
their blood.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:11:01.140
With six alternating hearts — two more than
the hagfish — the Ichtyoserpens has incredible

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:06.360
stamina, and can catch most anything that
enters their shadowy domain.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.520
But not all of Falora is gloomy swampland.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:14.240
In a sunny region further inland, a stunning
landscape has emerged.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.480
These tree-analogues are called megacherries,
named for the giant fruits that sprout from

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.180
the top of their trunk.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:26.500
These fruits take full advantage of planet
Falora’s low gravity, possessing a lightweight

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:30.070
internal structure to help them reach gargantuan
sizes.

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:36.060
While these fruits once served an evolutionary
purpose, their pollinator species has long

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.529
gone extinct.

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:44.240
As a result, despite the megacherries’ ability
to reproduce asexually, the species is slowly

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.060
disappearing.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:49.670
Yet most species of trees on Falora are thriving.

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:54.680
At the point where the low-gravity forests
of Falora are the densest, and the trees are

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:59.089
the tallest, very little light reaches the
forest floor.

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:04.340
Here, various bottom-dwellers feast on the
detritus floating in from above.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:10.200
The native life in the forest of ghosts have
become almost fully translucent, mirroring

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.080
the denizens of the deep sea.

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:18.209
It’s a haunting sight, but even more fantastical
organisms might be waiting on the next leg

00:12:18.209 --> 00:12:20.940
of our journey...

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:26.740
At the edge of the Cluster, we enter into
the orbit of a lonely F-Class star.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:32.220
Compared to our sun, this star is hotter,
brighter, and considerably more massive.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:38.279
One wouldn’t expect to find complex life
upon the sun-scorched planets in this system.

00:12:38.279 --> 00:12:44.230
Yet upon the dry surface of a world called
Whiterain, life has found a foothold nonetheless.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:50.529
Flocking near the rare sources of water, primitive
crustacean-like creatures and bizarre sprawls

00:12:50.529 --> 00:12:55.690
of tangling worms etch out an existence under
the punishing white sunlight.

00:12:55.690 --> 00:13:01.430
Hollow, pipe-like organisms make up the base
of this food web, feeding on algae that sprouts

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:02.790
from the moisture.

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:07.620
Mollusk-like organisms are more complex — and
epitomize some of the first land-dwellers.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:15.399
Among the most active species are the swirling
blue worms — hunters with a flexible, keratin-like

00:13:15.399 --> 00:13:18.300
exoskeleton that prey on smaller creatures.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:24.360
Although life on Whiterain is relatively new,
taken as a whole, this ecosystem is rich with

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.430
early promise.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.240
On Whiterain, shade is life.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:33.600
The desert sands of the planet bleach under
the white summer sun.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:40.100
Despite adaptations against harsh UV radiation,
not much survives the direct daylight.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:45.350
Many organisms choose to burrow in the rock
and only come out at night.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:50.950
In other extreme regions of Whiterain, temperatures
reach such heights that many creatures must

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:55.350
seek shelter in the shade provided by the
cracks in the landscape.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:14:00.940
Within these canyons, critters like the multi-limbed
Crowned Ambulimax hunt for the Snakeworms

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.000
that feed on the fungal mats that grow here.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:10.000
In turn, the Ambulimax are preyed upon by
stealthy ambush predators like the fearsome

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:17.470
Stellaflorex, a voracious, starfish-like hunter
that is, in essence, a walking stomach.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:23.120
Above this unfolding drama, tall tangleblossom
stalks are the only lifeform that dare reach

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.649
for the daylight for photosynthesis.

00:14:25.649 --> 00:14:31.820
Due to the isolated nature of these ravines,
the crag ecosystems are incredibly varied,

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:36.800
with no single nook or cranny exactly the
same…

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:42.029
While heading towards our final destination,
a few other worlds in the Cluster demand a

00:14:42.029 --> 00:14:43.810
brief visit.

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:47.000
The small world of Monas is our first.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:52.820
Mostly composed of deserts and sparse forests,
Monas isn’t a particularly enviable planet

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:54.240
to survive on.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:59.740
Bizarre sail trees have evolved to take up
as much light from the weak sun as possible,

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:06.089
before the planet’s dim second sun — which
has grown so inactive it is almost a gas giant,

00:15:06.089 --> 00:15:09.540
plunges the surface into the darkness of midyear.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:14.400
On a world like Monas, only a scarce few organisms
can survive…

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:19.120
The old planet of Spon is our second short
stop.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:25.740
Here, we can see a pale tripede fail to pull
off an ambush on a wary springspore.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:30.950
This fungoid escapes threats by launching
up into the atmosphere through a biological

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:34.500
mechanism that involves ejecting part of their
body.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:40.440
They will drift easily in the thick atmosphere
and grow a new foot in a safer spot.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:46.310
No trees sway in the wind of this planet;
instead, gigantic colonies of microbes have

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:49.980
taken over the tree-niche on the unique world
of Spon…

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:55.050
And our third short visit is to the planet
Karasma.

00:15:55.050 --> 00:16:00.330
Despite the intense rays of Karasma's sun,
ancient organisms called Dishtrees grow to

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.769
huge sizes on the surface.

00:16:02.769 --> 00:16:08.470
Their secret is a highly reflective coating
that reduces the star's harmful radiation

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:13.040
but allows specific wavelengths to be used
for remarkable growth.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:18.510
These living landmarks provide invaluable
cover to lifeforms incapable of surviving

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:25.290
the bright daylight, who spend their lives
in the shade of these helpful giants…

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:31.779
At the end of our journey across the cosmos,
we descend at last upon the planet Holos,

00:16:31.779 --> 00:16:34.640
perhaps the most extreme realm in the Cluster.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:41.120
Another tidally-locked world, much of the
ice planet Holos is blanketed in eternal darkness.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:46.930
On the surface, the only sunlight comes in
the form of reflected light from the planet’s

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:47.930
moon.

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:52.720
Yet periodically illuminating the glacial
landscape, are the vivid colors of bioluminescent

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.029
organisms.

00:16:54.029 --> 00:16:59.900
While most species exposed to the open atmosphere
quickly freeze solid, under the layers of

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:03.740
ice, a mysterious underworld is hiding.

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:08.890
Burrowing under the ice, we find the water
is surprisingly warm thanks to currents from

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:13.390
the sun-side transporting heat towards these
moonlit shallows.

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:19.289
Here, glowing aquatic lifeforms find shelter
amongst the skeletal remains of a crashed

00:17:19.289 --> 00:17:22.049
vessel of unknown origins.

00:17:22.049 --> 00:17:27.309
While we can only speculate as to the history
of this vessel, it stands as a reminder that

00:17:27.309 --> 00:17:34.210
when exploring deep space, one must always
navigate with caution, respect, and care.

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:40.330
Deeper in the depths of Holos, in the soft
glow of volcanic vents, simple organisms float

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:41.700
in the abyss.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:46.850
Through metabolizing the chemicals that seep
from the ocean floor, they can thrive even

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:49.330
in the total absence of sunlight.

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:55.230
On our planet, life itself might have begun
in the extreme heat and pressure of hydrothermal

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:56.370
vents.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:18:02.500
Likewise, these unforgiving vents are speculated
to be where life on Holos first emerged.

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:08.900
So, while these organisms might be simple,
they are perhaps the purest example of nature’s

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:10.629
unending struggle to endure...

00:18:10.629 --> 00:18:16.070
We’ve seen much on our long expedition through
The Cluster.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:21.289
Yet there are many more worlds that Charlotte
Veldhuizen has illustrated.

00:18:21.289 --> 00:18:25.850
If you enjoyed exploring these realms, you
can follow and support the artist using the

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:28.970
links in this video’s description.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:31.350
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:37.049
If you enjoyed this entry, please help make
these videos possible by liking, subscribing,

00:18:37.049 --> 00:18:42.080
and hitting the notification icon to stay
up to date on all things Curious.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:59.159
See you in the next video.

